\f0\fs24 \cf0 Custom Diarization:\
\
I\'92d do it as a separate local pipeline after transcription.\
\
High-level flow\
1. transcribe with WhisperX as you already do\
2. split audio into candidate speech chunks\
3. compute speaker embeddings for those chunks\
4. cluster embeddings into speaker groups\
5. assign each transcript segment to the nearest/overlapping cluster\
6. write a separate diarized transcript/report\
\
Concretely, the pipeline would look like this:\
\
## 1. Keep your current transcription pipeline\
Use your existing:\
- WhisperX transcription\
- alignment\
- cleaned transcript flow\
\
Don\'92t replace that part.\
\
## 2. Add a local diarization module\
Create a separate module, something like:\
- `diarization_local.py`\
\
Its job:\
- take local audio file\
- return speaker-labeled time intervals\
\
## 3. Segment the audio\
You need chunks of speech to compare.\
\
Two common ways:\
- use Whisper/WhisperX aligned transcript segments as the chunk boundaries\
- or use a VAD/speech segmentation tool first\
\
For your case, easiest is:\
- start from WhisperX aligned segments\
\
That gives each chunk:\
- start time\
- end time\
- transcript text\
\
## 4. Extract speaker embeddings\
For each chunk:\
- load the corresponding audio slice\
- run a SpeechBrain speaker embedding model\
- get a vector representing the speaker\'92s voice\
\
So each chunk becomes:\
- `(start, end, text, embedding_vector)`\
\
## 5. Cluster the embeddings\
Then cluster the vectors into speaker groups:\
- Speaker A\
- Speaker B\
- Speaker C\
\
Typical clustering approaches:\
- agglomerative clustering\
- spectral clustering\
- constrained clustering if you want min/max speakers\
\
At first, keep it simple:\
- agglomerative clustering\
- optional `min_speakers` / `max_speakers`\
\
## 6. Map clusters back to transcript segments\
Now assign each transcript segment:\
- `speaker_0`\
- `speaker_1`\
- etc.\
\
Then optionally apply your existing host-name heuristics:\
- known hosts per podcast\
- intro phrase detection\
- guest extraction\
\
That way:\
- cluster labels become real names where possible\
\
## 7. Produce separate output\
Write:\
- a diarized transcript text file\
- maybe also a CSV/JSON artifact\
\
For example:\
- `transcripts/<safe-title>.diarization.txt`\
- `transcripts/<safe-title>.diarization.csv`\
\
## Why this is less painful than NeMo\
Because you can bolt it onto your current system:\
- reuse transcript segments\
- reuse your naming heuristics\
- add one local embedding+clustering step\
\
Instead of adopting a much larger end-to-end stack.\
\
## What \'93least engineering pain overall\'94 means\
Even this is still custom work.\
The truly easiest route is still:\
- get access to the gated pyannote model\
- keep using WhisperX\'92s built-in diarization path\
\
Because then you avoid:\
- building your own segmentation+clustering glue\
- tuning cluster behavior\
- debugging speaker assignment quality\
\
So:\
- **SpeechBrain local path** = more realistic than NeMo on your Mac\
- **least engineering pain overall** = fix the pyannote access issue instead\
\
That\'92s the tradeoff.\
\
